# approval-auto.ps1

Auto-clicks the **Allow / Keep / Run Command** approval buttons in VS Code, across
every window and every chat session.

Built for the case where several agent chats are running at once and each one
stops to ask permission.

---

## What it does

Every second it scans VS Code's accessibility tree and:

1. Clicks any approval button it finds — `Allow`, `Allow in this Session`,
   `Always Allow`, `Run Command`, `Keep All Edits`, and about 40 more.
2. If no button is on screen, it looks in the **Sessions list** for a row whose
   status is `(Needs Input)`, clicks it to bring that session forward — then
   clicks its button on the next pass.

It never clicks the deny side: `Skip`, `Skip All`, `Cancel`, `Discard`, `Undo`,
`Don't Allow`, `Proceed without executing`.

---

## Read this first

These buttons are the human review step before VS Code runs a terminal command,
fetches a URL, or writes a file. Automating them means **nothing is reviewed**.

Only run it when you already trust what the agent is doing.

Two safer options exist for GitHub Copilot chat specifically:

- `chat.tools.autoApprove`, `chat.tools.terminal.autoApprove` in `settings.json`
- these are already turned on in this profile

The script mainly earns its keep for the **Claude Code panel**, which runs its
own `Allow` / `Skip` confirmation outside those settings.

---

## Setup

### 1. Requirements

| Need | Detail |
|---|---|
| OS | Windows 10 / 11 |
| Shell | Windows PowerShell 5.1 (`powershell.exe`) |
| Install | **None.** UI Automation ships with Windows |

No `dotnet`, no NuGet, no FlaUI. An earlier draft used FlaUI; it was dropped
because Windows already provides the same API.

### 2. Turn on the accessibility tree

VS Code is Electron. Chromium keeps its accessibility tree switched off until
something asks for it, so without this the script sees only Minimize / Restore /
Close.

Open `settings.json` (`Ctrl+Shift+P` → *Preferences: Open User Settings (JSON)*)
and add:

```jsonc
"editor.accessibilitySupport": "on"
```

Then **restart VS Code**.

> Already set in this profile at
> `C:\Users\z0054a6h\AppData\Roaming\Code\User\settings.json`

### 3. Verify

```powershell
powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File "copilot_helper_tools\approval-auto.ps1" -Diag
```

Expected output:

```
Windows attached:
  pid 12345   window  0x000A1B2C  Chrome_WidgetWin_1   luma_mcp_chatbot_repo - VS Code
  pid 12345   render  0x000A1B33  Chrome_RenderWidgetHostHWND
  ...
2 VS Code window(s), 5 handle(s) total.

Element counts per root:
  root 0:   842 elements,  97 clickable  [Chrome_WidgetWin_1]

Sessions currently waiting:
  Text         • 1 pending confirmation

Tree looks alive.
```

If element counts are near zero, see [Troubleshooting](#troubleshooting).

---

## How to run

Use a **separate terminal window** — not the one an agent is driving.

### Step 1 — see the rules

```powershell
powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File "copilot_helper_tools\approval-auto.ps1" -ShowTargets
```

Prints all rules in priority order, the waiting markers, and the deny list.
Rules shown in grey are off; `-IncludeAmbiguous` turns them on.

### Step 2 — see what is on screen

Open a real approval prompt first, then:

```powershell
powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File "copilot_helper_tools\approval-auto.ps1" -List
```

- **Green** `<-- WOULD CLICK` — an approval button it will press
- **Yellow** `<-- WAITING SESSION` — a marker it will click to focus a session
- Plain — ignored

Labels are shown normalized, e.g. `Allow (Ctrl+Enter)   -> 'Allow'`.

### Step 3 — dry run

```powershell
powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File "copilot_helper_tools\approval-auto.ps1" -DryRun
```

Watches continuously, reports what it *would* press, clicks nothing.
Let it run through a couple of real prompts before trusting it.

### Step 4 — run for real

```powershell
powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File "copilot_helper_tools\approval-auto.ps1"
```

Recommended everyday form:

```powershell
powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File "copilot_helper_tools\approval-auto.ps1" -IncludeOffscreen -LogFile approvals.log
```

`Ctrl+C` to stop.

---

## Options

| Flag | Default | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| `-Diag` | — | Show windows, sessions, element counts. Exit. |
| `-ShowTargets` | — | Show all rules and the deny list. Exit. |
| `-List` | — | Show what is on screen now. Exit. |
| `-AllTypes` | off | With `-List`: show every control type, not just clickable. |
| `-DryRun` | off | Watch and report, never click. |
| `-IncludeOffscreen` | off | Also click buttons scrolled out of view. |
| `-IncludeAmbiguous` | off | Also click bare `Run`, `Accept`, `Continue`, `Keep Going`. |
| `-NoFocusSessions` | off | Do not click waiting markers to switch session. |
| `-IntervalMs` | `1000` | Milliseconds between scans. |
| `-CooldownMs` | `2500` | Minimum gap before pressing the same label again. |
| `-SessionCooldownMs` | `6000` | Minimum gap before re-focusing the same session. |
| `-RescanWindowsMs` | `15000` | How often to look for new VS Code windows. |
| `-MaxClicks` | `0` | Stop after N clicks. `0` = forever. |
| `-LogFile` | — | Append every click to this file with a timestamp. |
| `-ExtraPatterns` | `@()` | Extra regex patterns to treat as approve buttons. |
| `-ExcludePatterns` | `@()` | Extra regex patterns to never click. |
| `-ProcessNames` | `Code`, `Code - Insiders` | Which editor processes to watch. |

### About `-IncludeAmbiguous`

Five rules are off by default: `Continue`, `Run`, `Accept`, `Keep Going`,
`Approve Plan Only`.

VS Code uses those exact same labels for the debug toolbar, the test explorer,
the merge editor and inline suggestions — controls that sit on screen all the
time. Turning this on can fire the wrong button. Use `-DryRun` first.

---

## Examples

Click once, then quit — good for a single test:

```powershell
powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File "copilot_helper_tools\approval-auto.ps1" -MaxClicks 1
```

React faster, keep a log:

```powershell
powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File "copilot_helper_tools\approval-auto.ps1" `
    -IntervalMs 500 -LogFile approvals.log
```

Never touch edits, only approve tool calls:

```powershell
powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File "copilot_helper_tools\approval-auto.ps1" `
    -ExcludePatterns '^Keep', '^Accept'
```

Add a rule of your own:

```powershell
powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File "copilot_helper_tools\approval-auto.ps1" `
    -ExtraPatterns '^Yes, proceed$'
```

Only watch VS Code Insiders:

```powershell
powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File "copilot_helper_tools\approval-auto.ps1" `
    -ProcessNames "Code - Insiders"
```

---

## Troubleshooting

### Only Minimize / Restore / Close appear

The Chromium tree is asleep.

1. Confirm `"editor.accessibilitySupport": "on"` is in `settings.json`.
2. Close **all** VS Code windows, then relaunch from a terminal:
   ```powershell
   code --force-renderer-accessibility
   ```
3. Re-run with `-Diag`.

### A button is on screen but `-List` does not show it

It is probably drawn on a canvas rather than as a real element. UI Automation
cannot see canvas pixels; that case needs image matching instead.

### It finds the button but does not click

The script tries three ways in order:

1. `InvokePattern` — no mouse movement, works off screen
2. `SelectionItemPattern` — session rows and toolbar-style buttons
3. A real mouse click at the element's point, cursor restored afterwards

If all three fail, run with `-Verbose` to see which step gave up.

### Nothing happens at all

Run PowerShell at the **same privilege level** as VS Code. If VS Code runs
normally and PowerShell runs as Administrator, Windows blocks the UI Automation
calls silently.

### It keeps switching between chat sessions

Raise `-SessionCooldownMs`, or turn the behaviour off with `-NoFocusSessions`.

### A waiting session is never reached

It has no visible marker. Open the **Agent Sessions sidebar** — that is what
renders the `Needs attention` and `requires input` badges the script looks for.

---

## How it works

**Finding windows.** Electron owns every window from a single process, so
`Process.MainWindowHandle` returns only one handle however many windows are
open. The script calls `EnumWindows` across the desktop and keeps visible
`Chrome_WidgetWin*` windows whose PID belongs to a `Code` process, then attaches
to each one's `Chrome_RenderWidgetHostHWND` children.

**Waking the tree.** Chromium builds its accessibility tree only after a client
sends `WM_GETOBJECT` with `OBJID_CLIENT` to the render widget. The script sends
that on attach and waits 1.5s.

**Matching labels.** Real labels are templates plus keybinding hints:

```
Allow (Ctrl+Enter)                    -> Allow
Allow python in this Session          -> matches ^Allow .+ in this Session$
Split Editor Right (Ctrl+\) [Alt] ... -> Split Editor Right
```

So it strips the `[Alt] ...` tail, strips trailing `(...)` groups, then applies
regex rules in priority order. Fixed-string matching cannot work here.

**Priority.** Least privilege first: a one-off `Allow` is preferred over
`Allow in this Session`, which is preferred over `Always Allow`.

